### The Problem

```
Without load balancing:
┌──────────┐
│ 1 Million │ ──────► ┌──────────┐
│ Requests  │         │ 1 Server │ 💀 DEAD
└──────────┘         └──────────┘

With load balancing:
┌──────────┐         ┌──────────────┐         ┌──────────┐
│ 1 Million │ ──────► │ Load         │ ──────► │ Server 1 │ (333K)
│ Requests  │         │ Balancer     │ ──────► │ Server 2 │ (333K)
└──────────┘         │ (Nginx/ALB)  │ ──────► │ Server 3 │ (333K)
                     └──────────────┘         └──────────┘
```

### Load Balancing Algorithms

```
1. Round Robin (Most Common)
   Request 1 → Server A
   Request 2 → Server B
   Request 3 → Server C
   Request 4 → Server A (back to start)

2. Least Connections
   → Send to server with fewest active connections
   → Good when requests take different amounts of time

3. IP Hash
   → Same user always goes to same server
   → Good for session-based apps

4. Weighted
   → Server A (powerful): gets 60% of traffic
   → Server B (weaker): gets 40% of traffic
```

### Nginx Load Balancer Configuration

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

# Define your servers
upstream fastapi_app {
    # Round robin (default)
    server 192.168.1.101:8000;
    server 192.168.1.102:8000;
    server 192.168.1.103:8000;

    # Or weighted:
    # server 192.168.1.101:8000 weight=3;
    # server 192.168.1.102:8000 weight=2;
    # server 192.168.1.103:8000 weight=1;

    # Or least connections:
    # least_conn;
    # server 192.168.1.101:8000;
    # server 192.168.1.102:8000;
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name api.yourapp.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://fastapi_app;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }
}
```
